<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uesugi Kenshin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1530">1530</a>–<a href="/years/1578">1578</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Nagao Kagetora</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uesugi Kenshin was a warlord of Echigo province in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a>, famous for his generalship and Buddhist devotion. He fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> in five battles at Kawanakajima between 1553 and 1564 and campaigned repeatedly against the Later Hojo in the Kanto. He died in 1578 while preparing a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin.jpg" alt="Uesugi Kenshin" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uesugi_Kenshin_Portrait_from_Uesugi_Shrine.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kenshin was born Nagao Kagetora, the third or fourth son of Nagao Tamekage, whose family served the Yamanouchi branch of the Uesugi clan. His father was killed in Etchū in December 1536, and his eldest brother, Nagao Harukage, took control of the clan. Kagetora was sent to the Rinsen-ji temple, where he spent the years from 7 to 14 on study, martial arts and Zen. He took control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagao-clan">Nagao clan</a> in 1548 and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasugayama-castle">Kasugayama Castle</a>.</p><p>In 1551 the Uesugi lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-norimasa">Uesugi Norimasa</a>, driven out of the Kantō by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-ujiyasu">Hōjō Ujiyasu</a>, took refuge with him. Kenshin gave shelter on the terms that Norimasa adopt him as heir and give him the title Lord of Echigo and the post of Kantō Kanrei, the shōgun's deputy. His name changed with his offices, to Uesugi Masatora, then Terutora, and finally Kenshin after he became a Buddhist monk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1553 two Shinano lords asked Kenshin to halt the advance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a>. The two men fought five times at Kawanakajima, in 1553, 1555, 1557, 1561 and 1564. Only the fourth battle, in 1561, was a serious all-out fight; it is called the costliest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a>, and scholars disagree on who won. Kenshin also campaigned against the Hōjō, besieging Odawara Castle in 1560–1561, and spent much of his life involved in the politics of Etchū.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiaki">Ashikaga Yoshiaki</a> in 1573, Kenshin broke his alliance with Nobunaga and joined those opposed to him. He took Nanao Castle in Noto and defeated an Oda army, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibata-katsuie">Shibata Katsuie</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tedorigawa">Battle of Tedorigawa</a> in 1577. He died in the spring of 1578, while preparing a grand campaign against Nobunaga, of esophageal cancer according to the theory most scholars accept.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kenshin's death poem reads, in one English rendering, that a life of forty-nine years is passed in a dream. He is remembered as the Dragon of Echigo and as a model of honourable conduct, and he is said to have wept on hearing of Shingen's death. When the Hōjō cut off the salt supplies to Kai, Kenshin sent salt to Shingen, saying that he did not fight with salt but with the sword, though he also let Echigo merchants sell salt in Takeda territory. His rule is credited with fostering local industries and trade in Echigo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uesugi Kenshin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hojo Ujiyasu won a celebrated victory at Kawagoe in 1546 and expanded across the Kanto, resisting attacks by <b>Uesugi Kenshin</b> and Takeda Shingen.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-hojo-clan-domain">Later Hojo clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Shingen conquered Shinano and parts of Suruga, Kozuke and Totomi, fighting <b>Uesugi Kenshin</b> five times at Kawanakajima.</blockquote><a href="/state/takeda-clan-domain">Takeda clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nagao Kagetora</b>, later Uesugi Kenshin, took over the clan in 1548 and secured the province by about 1551, ruling from Kasugayama castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/uesugi-clan-domain">Uesugi clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasugayama-castle">Kasugayama Castle</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasugayama-castle">Kasugayama Castle</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.15,138.21&amp;year=1561" aria-label="Kasugayama Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8011 826 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Uesugi Kenshin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8461 1096h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8461 1096h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.15,138.21&amp;year=1561">What was here in 1561? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
